                <p class=" coming-soon-regular">
                    Addition formula for Montgomery Curve (Affine):
                    <br>
                </p>
                <p class=" coming-soon-regular" style="background-color: rgba(151, 240, 42, 0.13);">
Input: P, Q &isin; E(F<sub>p</sub>) with P &ne; Q<br>
Output: R = (P + Q) &isin; E(F<sub>p</sub>)<br><br>

(x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>), (x<sub>2</sub>, y<sub>2</sub>) = P, Q<br>
&alpha; = (y<sub>2</sub> &minus; y<sub>1</sub>) / (x<sub>2</sub> &minus; x<sub>1</sub>)<br>
x<sub>3</sub> = B&alpha;<sup>2</sup> &minus; A &minus; x<sub>1</sub> &minus; x<sub>2</sub><br>
y<sub>3</sub> = &alpha;(x<sub>1</sub> &minus; x<sub>3</sub>) &minus; y<sub>1</sub><br>
R = (x<sub>3</sub>, y<sub>3</sub>)<br>
                </p>
                <code class=" coming-soon-regular">
def montgomery_add(P, Q, mod, A, B):
    y2_y1 = (Q[1] - P[1]) % mod
    x2_x1 = (Q[0] - P[0]) % mod
    alpha = (y2_y1 * pow(x2_x1, -1, mod)) % mod
    x3 = B * pow(alpha,2, mod) - A - P[0] - Q[0]
    y3 = alpha * (P[0] - x3) - P[1]
    return(x3 % mod, y3 % mod)                                                                               
                </code>


                <p class=" coming-soon-regular">
                    <br>
                        Doubling formula for Montgomery Curve (Affine)
                        <br>
                </p>
                <p class=" coming-soon-regular" style="background-color: rgba(151, 240, 42, 0.13);">
    Input: P &isin; E(F<sub>p</sub>)<br>
    Output: R = [2]P &isin; E(F<sub>p</sub>)<br><br>

    (x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>) = P<br>
    &alpha; = (3x<sub>1</sub>&sup2; + 2A x<sub>1</sub> + 1) / (2B y<sub>1</sub>)<br>
    x<sub>3</sub> = B&alpha;&sup2; &minus; A &minus; 2x<sub>1</sub><br>
    y<sub>3</sub> = &alpha;(x<sub>1</sub> &minus; x<sub>3</sub>) &minus; y<sub>1</sub><br>
    R = (x<sub>3</sub>, y<sub>3</sub>)<br>
                </p>
                <code class=" coming-soon-regular">
def montgomery_double(P, mod, A, B):
    num = (3 * pow(P[0],2, mod) + 2 * A * P[0] + 1) % mod
    den = pow(2 * B * P[1], -1, mod)
    alpha = (num * den) % mod
    x3 = B * pow(alpha, 2, mod) - A - 2 * P[0]
    y3 = alpha * (P[0] - x3) - P[1]
    return (x3 % mod, y3 % mod)                       
                </code>


                <p class=" coming-soon-regular">
                    <br>
                        Montgomery’s binary algorithm in the group E(F<sub>p</sub>)
                        <br>
                </p>
                <p class=" coming-soon-regular" style="background-color: rgba(151, 240, 42, 0.13);">
    Input: P &isin; E(F<sub>p</sub>) and an n-bit integer k = &sum; 2<sup>i</sup>k<sub>i</sub> where k<sub>n&minus;1</sub> = 1<br>
    Output: [k]P &isin; E(F<sub>p</sub>)<br><br>

    1. Set (R<sub>0</sub>, R<sub>1</sub>) to (P, [2]P)<br>
    2. for i = n &minus; 2 down to 0 do<br>
    3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;If k<sub>i</sub> = 0 then<br>
    4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Set (R<sub>0</sub>, R<sub>1</sub>) to ([2]R<sub>0</sub>, R<sub>0</sub> + R<sub>1</sub>)<br>
    5.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Else:<br>
    6.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Set (R<sub>0</sub>, R<sub>1</sub>) to (R<sub>0</sub> + R<sub>1</sub>, [2]R<sub>1</sub>)<br>
    7. Return R<sub>0</sub><br>

                </p>
                <code class=" coming-soon-regular">
def montgomery_binary(P, k, mod, A, B):
    R0, R1 = P, montgomery_double(P, mod, A, B)
    n = k.bit_length()
    for i in range(n-2 , -1, -1):
        if (k >> i) & 1 == 0:
            R0, R1 = montgomery_double(R0, mod, A, B), montgomery_add(R0, R1, mod, A, B)
        else:
            R0, R1 = montgomery_add(R0, R1, mod, A, B), montgomery_double(R1, mod, A, B)
    return R0                                                  
                </code>

